Below is a minimal, step-by-step example that shows how to add a **hillshade** layer that draws from a `raster-dem` source in a MapLibre GL JS map.  
You can copy-paste it into a simple HTML page, adjust the URLs and options to match your own DEM tiles, and you’ll see a shaded-relief rendering on top of the base map.

---

## 1. Basic HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // JavaScript goes here (see next section)
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Tip:** Use the latest MapLibre GL JS version (the CDN link above points to `4.4.0` at the time of writing). Adjust the version if you need a newer release.

---

## 2. Create the map and add the DEM source

```js
// 1️⃣ Initialise the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // Base map (you can replace this with any raster/vector source you like)
      "osm": {
        type: "raster",
        tiles: [
          "https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
          "https://b.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
          "https://c.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
        ],
        tileSize: 256,
        attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
      }
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: "osm-basemap",
        type: "raster",
        source: "osm"
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],   // longitude, latitude
  zoom: 2
});
```

### Add the DEM source

```js
// 2️⃣ Add a raster-DEM source (replace the URL template with your own DEM tiles)
map.addSource('dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // Example: MapTiler DEM tiles (public, limited usage)
  // You can also use your own tiles, e.g. from a self-hosted TileServer-GL or a Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF endpoint.
  tiles: [
    'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,
  // Optional: set the maximum zoom level that the DEM tiles cover.
  maxzoom: 14,
  // Optional: if your DEM tiles are encoded as Mapbox Terrain-RGB, set this flag.
  // MapLibre will decode the RGB values to elevation automatically.
  // (If you serve plain 16-bit grayscale tiles, set `encoding: "mapbox"` to false.)
  // encoding: "mapbox" // default, keep unless you know otherwise
});
```

> **Why `raster-dem`?**  
> The `raster-dem` source type tells MapLibre that the tiles contain elevation data (not visual imagery). The engine will decode the values and make them available to the hillshade layer.

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

```js
// 3️⃣ Add the hillshade layer (must be added *after* the DEM source is ready)
map.on('load', () => {
  // Optional: add a terrain layer if you also want 3-D extrusion / pitch-tilt.
  // map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'hillshade',
    type: 'hillshade',
    source: 'dem',
    // The hillshade layer does not need a separate source-layer name.
    // Paint properties control the visual appearance.
    paint: {
      // Light direction (azimuth) – 0° = north, 90° = east, etc.
      'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
      // Light intensity (0-1)
      'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'viewport', // or 'map' to keep light fixed to north
      // Adjust contrast of the shading
      'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,
      // Optional: tweak the colors
      // 'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
      // 'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
      // 'hillshade-accent-color': '#777777'
    }
  });
});
```

### What the paint properties do

| Property | Meaning | Typical range |
|----------|---------|---------------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | Azimuth of the simulated light source (degrees clockwise from north). | 0-360 (e.g., 315 gives a north-west light). |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | Whether the light direction stays fixed to the viewport (`viewport`) or to the map north (`map`). | `"viewport"` (default) or `"map"`. |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | Multiplies the elevation values before shading; higher values make relief look more dramatic. | 0-1 (0 = flat, 1 = full elevation). |
| `hillshade-shadow-color`, `hillshade-highlight-color`, `hillshade-accent-color` | Override the default grayscale shading colors. | Any CSS color string. |

---

## 4. (Optional) Add 3-D terrain

If you also want to enable true 3-D terrain (so the map can be pitched and the surface appears raised), call `setTerrain` after the DEM source is added:

```js
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'dem',
  // Exaggerate the heights to make mountains more visible.
  exaggeration: 1.5
});
```

> **Note:** The hillshade layer works independently of the terrain layer; you can have one, both, or neither.

---

## 5. Full working example (copy-paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        osm: {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: [
            'https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
            'https://b.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
            'https://c.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'osm-basemap', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: -17
  });

  // Add DEM source (replace with your own DEM tiles)
  map.addSource('dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    tiles: [
      // MapTiler Terrain-RGB (public demo key – replace with your own key)
      'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'
    ],
    tileSize: 256,
    maxzoom: 14
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // Optional 3-D terrain
    // map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // Hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'viewport',
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY` with a valid API key (or point the `tiles` array at your own DEM tile server). The map will display the OpenStreetMap raster base, a hillshade overlay, and you can tilt/pitch the view to see the relief.

---

## Common pitfalls & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears** | DEM source not loaded (404, CORS, wrong URL) | Open the browser dev-tools → Network tab, verify the DEM tiles are fetched successfully. Ensure the server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| **Hillshade looks flat (no shading)** | `hillshade-exaggeration` set to `0` or very low | Increase `hillshade-exaggeration` (e.g., `0.5`-`1`). |
| **Colors look wrong** | Using a DEM that isn’t encoded as Mapbox Terrain-RGB but as plain grayscale | Set `encoding: "mapbox"` to `false` when adding the source, or convert your tiles to Terrain-RGB. |
| **Map tilts but terrain stays flat** | Terrain not enabled (`setTerrain` not called) | Call `map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1 })` after the DEM source is ready. |
| **CORS error when loading DEM tiles** | Tile server doesn’t allow cross-origin requests | Configure the server to send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or the specific origin). For static file hosts (e.g., GitHub Pages, S3), enable CORS in the bucket or site settings. |
| **Performance drops when zoomed in** | DEM tiles are high-resolution and many requests are made | Reduce `maxzoom` on the DEM source, or serve a lower-resolution DEM for zoom levels > 12. You can also enable `tileSize: 512` if your server provides 512-pixel tiles. |

---

### Quick checklist

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** with a valid tile URL template.  
2. **Add a `hillshade` layer** that references that source.  
3. (Optional) **Set terrain** with `map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: … })`.  
4. Adjust **paint properties** (`hillshade-illumination-direction`, `hillshade-exaggeration`, etc.) to get the visual style you want.  
5. Verify **CORS** and **tile availability**; check the network tab if nothing shows up.  

That’s all you need to get hillshading working in a MapLibre GL JS style! Happy mapping.
